// SHAPE: `rock_shader(<entry>)` inside a FN BODY x nobox representation.
//
// The rejection this pins is SPURIOUS. `rock_shader` is a compile-time expansion:
// its argument lowers to the entry's NAME as a string literal (`do return
// ("shape_reg_frag") end`, read off the emitted Lua), and what reaches the engine
// is `__rock_shader_register("shape_reg_frag", [[<glsl>]])`. The fn is never taken
// as a value at runtime, so the ABI-disagreement hazard
// `reject_nobox_fn_taken_by_value` exists to prevent cannot arise here.
//
// It fires anyway once `Vec2` is marked, because a fullscreen entry's signature
// always mentions `Vec2` (the TexCoord varying) and the check matches any
// `Expr::Global(name)` naming a function, without excluding a compile-time
// expansion's argument position.
//
// The discriminant is the SPELLING, not the marking: the global-initializer form
// in `shader_registration_global_init.rock` emits clean under the same marking, and
// that is the form every one of the ~20 real in-tree sites uses
// (`stone/engine/rendering/draw.rock`). So this costs no in-tree annotation today.
// What it is evidence for is that one expression rejects in a fn body and emits at
// global scope.
//
// The verdict lands at EMIT, so the surface is `rock compile` -- `rock_shader` is a
// stone-engine global and a bare `rock test` would die at `__rock_shader_register
// (a nil value)` before the shape could be judged.
//
// Corpus cases: `shader_registration_fn_body_unmarked` (control),
// `shader_registration_fn_body_vec2_marked` (the spurious rejection).

@shader(fragment)
fn shape_reg_frag(tc: Vec2) -> Vec4 {
    return Vec4 { x: tc.x, y: tc.y, z: 0.0, w: 1.0 }
}

fn shape_reg_use() -> string { return rock_shader(shape_reg_frag) }
